  <h1>How Dog Bite Claims Work</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Aspen Lockwood (AL), Editor-in-Chief. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>The legal basis for your claim</h2><p>In strict liability states, the dog owner is liable for bite injuries as a matter of law &mdash; no need to prove they were negligent. In one-bite rule states, you must show the owner knew or should have known the dog was dangerous. In all states, negligence (failure to restrain, leash law violation) provides an alternative theory.</p>
  <h2>Filing the insurance claim</h2><p>The dog owner&rsquo;s homeowners or renters insurance is the primary source of recovery. File a notice of claim with the insurer promptly. The adjuster will investigate, request medical records, and make a settlement offer. Initial offers are almost always lower than fair value.</p>
  <h2>What drives settlement value</h2><p>Medical costs and future treatment needs carry the most weight. Visible scarring, especially facial scarring, dramatically increases value. The victim&rsquo;s occupation matters (a hand model vs. a desk worker for hand injuries). Children&rsquo;s claims often settle for more because emotional trauma is presumed more severe.</p>
  <h2>Negotiation and litigation</h2><p>Most dog bite claims settle without filing a lawsuit. The attorney sends a demand letter to the insurer with medical records and supporting evidence. Insurers negotiate to a number within policy limits. If the insurer undervalues the claim or the injury exceeds policy limits, a lawsuit is filed.</p>
